 seat: Mike DeWine (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Ohio's open governor's race pairs Democrat Amy Acton against Republican Vivek Ramaswamy in a state that leans Republican by about five points at baseline. With Mike DeWine term-limited, neither candidate carries incumbency, and the contest has been rated a Tossup by every major race rater through late September. Recent polls show Acton narrowly ahead, including a September BGSU/YouGov survey with her up three among likely voters, though earlier surveys were mixed and some showed Ramaswamy slightly in front.\n\nRamaswamy holds a large financial advantage, with roughly $30.7 million on hand compared to Acton's $8.1 million, giving him the resources to define the race down the stretch. Prediction markets nonetheless price Acton at about 60 percent, and headlines in late September describe her momentum. The race remains genuinely competitive, with the Covid record, health costs, and gas prices as central themes.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.545, "margin": 0.49}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5929, "margin": 1.0}]}